# What TLC must report for each model configuration in this directory.
# scripts/ci/tlc.sh runs every *.cfg and fails unless the result is exactly this.
#
#   <config> pass                 TLC finds no error
#   <config> fail <Name>          TLC reports that invariant or property violated
#
# The "fail" lines are designs TLC rejected, or a stated limit of the design in
# the code. They stay so that every run shows the checker failing on a known-bad
# design: a spec that cannot express the bug proves nothing.

# The design in the code: one writer per step. A cancelled run's turn writes
# nothing; cancel() waits for the turns, records what is still running, closes the root.
chain.single-writer pass
fan.single-writer pass
# ...also when a turn does not return before cancel()'s wait runs out.
chain.single-writer-timeout pass
fan.single-writer-timeout pass

# The LIMIT of that design. These drop the assumption that a tracker write which
# had already begun lands within cancel()'s wait (5 s in the code; a bd write
# takes about 0.2 s). The "running" write is now a compare-and-set (StartCAS,
# bd --if-status open), so a late START is refused; a late RESULT write still
# is not fenced, and that is what these show.
chain.single-writer-slow-write fail StepResultIsFinal
fan.single-writer-slow-write fail StepResultIsFinal

# cancel() as it was before vibesync-6zos.3: it records the steps itself and
# does not wait for the turns it stopped.
chain.main fail StepResultIsFinal
fan.main fail StepResultIsFinal
# The same, with the turn checking "was my step settled?" before it judges.
chain.fence-before-judge fail StepResultIsFinal
fan.fence-before-judge fail StepResultIsFinal
# The same check made as one atomic check-and-write.
chain.fence-atomic-settled fail StepResultIsFinal
fan.fence-atomic-settled fail StepResultIsFinal

# An atomic check-and-write that also reads the run's cancelled flag. It needs a
# compare-and-set on status that bd does not have, and it is NOT sound: with step
# start modelled as admit / re-check / mark running (review on #188), a step admitted
# just before the flag is left in_progress under the closed root (fan shape). Chain
# runs one step at a time, so nothing can be admitted while cancel() runs.
chain.fence-atomic-cancelling pass
fan.fence-atomic-cancelling fail ClosedRootHasOnlyClosedSteps

# What the model shows about the two parts of the design in the code (corrected after CodeRabbit on PR #188): the
# full root-close sweep IS shown necessary (weakening it makes fan.single-writer-snapshot-swept fail); fresh
# cancellation records are NOT shown necessary while the sweep is in place (snapshot recording on the current design
# passes). Fresh records stay because they record each running step as cancelled, rather than leaving it to the sweep.
# The snapshot design AS IT WAS (before PR #188's review): the root close swept only open steps and the start was not a
# compare-and-set. Under those flags, recording only the steps read at the start leaves a step in_progress under the
# closed root. This configuration records the old design; it does NOT show the snapshot change alone is unsafe now.
fan.single-writer-snapshot fail ClosedRootHasOnlyClosedSteps
# The snapshot recording ISOLATED on the current design (full sweep, conditional start): it holds, because the root
# close sweeps the step the snapshot missed. If the sweep is ever weakened, this one fails (CodeRabbit on PR #188).
fan.single-writer-snapshot-swept pass
# cancel() refusing a run with nothing in_progress, and giving it back (82fd087): a turn that had already seen
# the flag gave its step up, and the run is left with a step whose try is used but which never ran.
# Found by the outside review of PR #188 (Fable); needs cancel modelled as claim, then decide.
chain.single-writer-cancel-refuse fail NoStrandedStep
# No "cancelled?" check between the prompt render and the turn start, in the design in the
# code. It PASSES: with the compare-and-set start and the root close sweeping in_progress
# steps, a late start is refused or swept, so the model's step and root properties hold
# without the check. What the check still prevents is a SESSION starting after a cancel,
# which the model does not track; the unit tests cover that (cancel-race.test.ts).
fan.single-writer-timeout-no-recheck pass
